To address the challenge of designing a heterogeneous stereoselective butadiene polymerization catalyst, we were inspired by molecular cyclopentadienyl \((\mathrm{Cp})\) catalysts. Based on the established isolobal analogy between \(\mathrm{Cp}\) and tris-pyrazolylborate \((\mathrm{Tp})\) complexes, we reasoned that the material known as MFU-4/ \((\mathrm{Zn}_{5}\mathrm{Cl}_{4}(\mathrm{BTDD})_{3},\) \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BTDD}\) = bis(1H-1,2,3-triazolo[4,5-b],[4',5'- i])dibenzo[1,4]dioxin), which features exchangeable \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\) cations in a Tp-like coordination environment would serve as an excellent support for addressing this challenge (Figure 1). Through this approach, we identified CoMFU-4/ (1) as a highly selective catalyst for the 1,4-cis-polymerization \((>\!99\ \ \%)\) of 1,3-butadiene. To our knowledge, this stereoselectivity is the highest reported for 1,3-butadiene polymerization with a heterogeneous catalyst. We further present evidence for the single-site structure of 1 as well as a molecular model for interrogating the mechanistic basis for cis-selectivity in this catalyst.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Stereoselective catalysis often relies upon systematic variation as a means to optimize small differences in activa
tion energy, which can be difficult to anticipate a priori but nevertheless underlie significant differences in stereoselectivity. In analogy to metalloligand-based catalysis, cation exchange provides a unique opportunity to compare the reactivity of a range of transition metal cations with equivalent coordination geometry in the solid state. By contrast, conventional heterogeneous supports do not show equivalent support-catalyst interaction across a broad range of transition metals, highlighting the advantage that the well-defined, modular structure of MOFs hold for heterogeneous catalyst design.
Given the variety of transition metals used for stereoselective diene polymerization in Cp complexes, Error! Bookmark not defined: we initiated our catalyst development by evaluating a variety of 3d metals. Thus, MFU-4/ samples exchanged with Ti, Cr, Fe, Co, and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{16}\) were treated with 1,3-butadiene and modified methylaminoxane-12 (MMAO-12) under semi-batch conditions. The results evidenced considerable variation in activity and selectivity based on both the choice of transition metal and its oxidation state (Table 1). In all cases, the exchanged transition metals were responsible for catalysis, the all-zinc MFU-4/ parent MOF showing no background reactivity for this reaction. Surprisingly, the metal-exchanged MFU-4/ catalysts previously reported to be most active for ethylene polymerization (i.e. Ti-, and Cr-exchanged MFU-4/), were found to be least effective for 1,3-butadiene polymerization (Table 1, entries 2–5, 8). Thus, although Ti(IV)-MFU-4/ showed promising activity for 1,3-butadiene polymerization, the resulting polymer is predominantly trans and exhibits modest stereopurity (Table 1, entry 2). This observation contrasts with the well documented 1,4-cis selectivity of \(\mathrm{CpTiCl}_{3}\). Error! Bookmark not defined: underscoring the somewhat empirical nature of stereoselective catalysis. MFU-4/ samples exchanged with late transition metals show contrasting reactivity with both olefins and dienes: whereas Fe(II)-MFU-4/ is unreactive towards ethylene and produces polybutadiene with only modest stereoselectivity \((68\%\ 1,4\mathrm{-}c i s)\), Ni(II)-MFU-4/ is a selective ethylene dimerization catalyst and a very active 1,3-butadiene polymerization catalyst, providing a polymer with promising stereoselectivity \((96\%\ 1,4\mathrm{-}c i s)\). However, Co(II)-MFU-4/ (1) provides polybutadiene with the highest stereoselectivity, greater than \(99\%\ 1,4\mathrm{-}c i s\) content (Table 1, entry 7). This selectivity is exceptional among heterogeneous catalysts and is relevant for high-performance elastomer applications, prompting a more in-depth study of the nature of the Co site in this catalyst.
